Equivalence Groups Found
└── Modbus TCP client for reading/writing holding registers to industrial field devices
    ├── Candidates : pymodbus, pyModbusTCP, modbus-tk
    ├── Used APIs  : ModbusTcpClient, client.connect, client.read_holding_registers, client.write_register, rr.isError, rr.registers
    ├── Pitfalls   : pyModbusTCP uses read_holding_registers()/write_single_register() returning lists/bool instead of a response object with .isError() and 
    │   .registers, requiring rewrite of error-checking logic; modbus-tk requires building a TcpMaster and calling execute() with explicit function codes 
    │   (cst.READ_HOLDING_REGISTERS etc.), a more verbose and structurally different API; unit/slave id parameter naming and defaults differ across libraries 
    │   (unit= vs slave= vs implicit); error handling model differs: pymodbus returns error objects, pyModbusTCP returns None on failure, modbus-tk raises 
    │   exceptions
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── All three are Python Modbus master/client libraries capable of TCP communication with PLC/RTU devices, providing register read/write operations 
        analogous to pymodbus's ModbusTcpClient usage here (read_holding_registers/write_register). pyModbusTCP is TCP-specific and has a close functional 
        match (read_holding_registers, write_single_register). modbus-tk supports TCP master mode via execute() with function codes, offering the same core 
        capability though with a different call pattern.

